#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "b10aaa3e-2ba4-51af-be3b-056d72692885" :question/math-academy-id "e-10889" :question/problem "![](images/23/23e7d18cc240e7cf3937d11fd6484c3cce1027c237c85430f69f17bf25f0f487.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is given above. Find $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac32.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in [1,2].$ Notice that $x=\\dfrac32$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/90/9067715198a510cc1399bb257352babb1ac093db1c80c611c2236c10b43607bc.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(1,2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-2}{2-1}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096376 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9811f16e-75aa-5982-b97b-cc59b33fb7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10890" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6ae60cb8606fb75aa49251ad894ce6a56173d6d5310aba0018e6c6933d1b93fe.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y = f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find $h'(-1)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(x) + g'(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(-1) =f'(-1) + g'(-1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in(-2,1)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in(-2,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c6ff6240abc7b56f058547e29ef250514fd19723057a65089ef0fa6da0ec044.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in(-2,1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{1-(-1)}{1-(-2)} = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(-1)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in(-2,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta g}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{-1-2}{0-(-2)} = -\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g'(-1)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & ={f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "1fb2491b-f442-57e3-990f-65bf1270d847" :question/math-academy-id "e-10891" :question/problem "![](images/69/69dd5f35d420746ebecc4fc7b01f09f0be01ccbc9b02cc3987533be5c0ec58ad.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find $h'(0)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot g'(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(0)=f'(0)\\cdot g(0)+f(0)\\cdot g'(0).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(0)=1$ and $g(0)=-1.$ \n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(-1,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, $f'(0)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ on $x\\in(-0.5,1)$ equals $2.$ So, $g'(0)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & ={f}^{′}(0)\\cdot g(0)+f(0)\\cdot {g}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096378 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5e929c25-68b1-5a6b-92a0-d12b90a112e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10892" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cd56facb5599cc8f2ffd8de8ee152cfec47844fc96d3daddb42e59afb0c8a57.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find $h'(2)$ where $h(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = \\dfrac{f'(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot g'(x)}{(g(x))^2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(2) = \\dfrac{f'(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot g'(2)}{(g(2))^2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(2)=1$ and $g(2)=0.5.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, $f'(2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ on $x\\in(1,3)$ equals $-0.5.$ So, $g'(2)=-0.5.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{{(g(2))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (0.5)-(1)\\cdot (-0.5)}{(0.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c62e13-678a-598d-b191-e515187394a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238348} {:answer/id #uuid "6a281104-3172-5207-85ac-d3523e9f06ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238976} {:answer/id #uuid "7523980a-3dc9-556f-a900-bd8d74b02fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239067} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5b7892-34f3-551d-a7a4-706e26b36bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240143} {:answer/id #uuid "fc55fbbe-3f5a-500d-a655-ae7d80f5fb9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21c62e13-678a-598d-b191-e515187394a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238348} :answer-field/id #uuid "16fa28a0-1995-57c0-b2e6-fa809fab21ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6c1e529-59cb-55e7-b759-671e209b110e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104785" :question/problem "![](images/df/df4fed951351dfc1203ba6257f404a5efadbec37b9f11098af1fc36a7793b0c7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=g(x)-f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)-{f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/81/814ce0fdce1428b3bd619caa5e8d09a828d2f2c52fd0b20b7f520562319fb848.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{4-1}{3-(-1)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{3-(-1)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35622553-ec3a-571d-9154-58bd121230ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238518} {:answer/id #uuid "b83b00bc-0ac3-5b8b-a8cc-3b01d437f685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239659} {:answer/id #uuid "cb549930-56b5-58ab-afec-ab7717bfe31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239836} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c96ede-23e9-5d02-8dec-ef47a8f02f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240061} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f90799-a3d9-5adb-a28f-7b8ae265ae83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb549930-56b5-58ab-afec-ab7717bfe31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239836} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dbca923-c686-5122-b696-d5906f3d500f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "720b1d63-4a80-5bc1-95a2-9593bdc22709" :question/math-academy-id "q-104803" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec9faab92da8de318d78e548adc35e8df20083816f8bafdacf0cfbbd497e084c.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule and the addition rule for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=0$ and $g(-2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)-{f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da76ba1-bd8d-5ac3-9e2e-00feea2bfdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238457} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3f188f-d5ab-5d97-918c-dce468688130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238735} {:answer/id #uuid "5b81d360-bb0c-5fc6-9590-993b528b33eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238848} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb454b2-293e-584d-8880-551707c4f7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239720} {:answer/id #uuid "cae711e6-3724-58f1-8dab-ff20071b2fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f3f188f-d5ab-5d97-918c-dce468688130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238735} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f85b27-e96c-5799-8d49-7eae7fe09dc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11dcc5e7-45ba-5891-b7c4-d5a2f0ec6038" :question/math-academy-id "q-105497" :question/problem "![](images/08/089281519cd8926eb187c67d465666838b8143b4dd86e081ef3c151613fd27fd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(4),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[3,5].$ Notice that $x=4$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/68/68daf42f2d14b0820bd36db73a8abe7b4d3d449c2b5b865b1d2affd70e069b43.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (3,5)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-3-0}{5-3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5056940e-4ed6-57c1-98d4-4fb3c997ca37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573519} {:answer/id #uuid "51c16e8a-861d-5b18-8212-02c138fd98e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573524} {:answer/id #uuid "594f4473-d5d3-5e62-ba23-fb73f10a2a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573539} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7db02d-2075-52b7-84d1-4b1556734474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573562} {:answer/id #uuid "c3378176-d404-5491-a008-d4a30c633beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51c16e8a-861d-5b18-8212-02c138fd98e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573524} :answer-field/id #uuid "b308e7df-567f-5cd4-b865-053b904c951e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c089daaf-37cc-52fb-a2a0-6074ff898dc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105501" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8345270e511644cfb019099b907ecadff056f7efbd396e84d4313f3c3ab5817.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)-2g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)-2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)-2{g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2,4)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (0,4)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09e4836acd54b814c1691c60003614d206fc2eee5cda6d7ad8d6222dd72d2d4f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{4-2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (0,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-0}{4-0}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)-2{g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75b7cdb7-77d7-57c2-97b3-ea13b000c118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b7cdb7-77d7-57c2-97b3-ea13b000c118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573577} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d75a63-b626-59fc-9980-1c756d4b224f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17f4d138-65e0-5f89-8826-9793c7584c9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344119" :question/problem "![](images/63/6337a487598d8b5e432301b1a0d9a64407323a6bdcfa0f2fa7ff2ee140fac1bd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(5).$\n\n${f}^{′}(5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in [4,6].$ Notice that $x=5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/42/42b4ddb29283047bd7ea8141ee14c793607b6b79a18f5d599a93839aec5a77b5.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (4,6)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{5-(-1)}{6-4}=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(5)=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc068b36-e83d-5fd2-9112-e63718de7e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc068b36-e83d-5fd2-9112-e63718de7e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573667} :answer-field/id #uuid "62da2b0e-6f79-559d-970c-50f59887f217" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b5f58b1-1ad2-5dc4-9868-fc9b1d7a5b07" :question/math-academy-id "q-344801" :question/problem "![](images/34/34d404457497fea78bd8b4e03d9ab9f4cc270487f1c478cb52780748e52163d5.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)={f}^{′}(1)+{g}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62c27964999163f87224059d85e5e6fe07a2e8b343e9fef1c34723bf9fbaaec1.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-(-2)}{2-(-1)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-1}{2-(-1)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & ={f}^{′}(1)+{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =1+(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c875190b-c18b-5e5f-88f4-f546edcab3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c875190b-c18b-5e5f-88f4-f546edcab3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573685} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e6f39a-f3fa-5af8-9dc4-36fec66a2e49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c31fc413-2779-5fad-acf9-ef1563b305fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-344908" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4fd9b46fdb02c23e04c50e437076d7a74f4823546b6608dbe537defb8a439fc.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=1$ and $g(-2)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1)}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffa62cc5-94c6-551b-96c6-16b6cd1270ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffa62cc5-94c6-551b-96c6-16b6cd1270ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573769} :answer-field/id #uuid "652c2813-5b15-52c4-be47-c3ffda7fdd27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44024ffd-d823-5830-bedd-a0c95dfca83c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344916" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5f9c20bfeb54e816c98e75469f873d242b26143bdab65e56706861d8cbd7384.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=3$ and $g(-2)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161bb233-e765-5131-bcaa-adffa0416fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327127} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd5d9e4-beb0-51fb-9fea-a74c90349602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327457} {:answer/id #uuid "49bf799e-1e94-56cd-a895-69a0915be472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327574} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc3dc39-6cac-523c-802f-a09df5948005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327905} {:answer/id #uuid "d72284db-2492-5ac0-bcc8-bb51498ad41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fc3dc39-6cac-523c-802f-a09df5948005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327905} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1c1979d-d233-51af-987b-580560b5e47b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a073ec9-0e41-5290-8f8f-28a860565103" :question/math-academy-id "q-48944" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b47240678a326854124fe4d52398bd6db8f2dd3826713ee7449b48e4891e7cc.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{7}{2})-f(\\frac{7}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{7}{2}))}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(\\frac{7}{2})=g(\\frac{7}{2})=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (3,4)$ equals $-2.$ So, ${f}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})=-2.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})g(\\frac{7}{2})-f(\\frac{7}{2}){g}^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{7}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a4551c2-b59b-5eca-8184-42852e416633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573474} {:answer/id #uuid "5eca6fcd-90ba-5a2f-8d34-1b77aaafcabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573543} {:answer/id #uuid "777a7f91-b566-5e10-aaf6-e26f1de7019a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573579} {:answer/id #uuid "bf15a1e8-e7a4-5205-ae23-b7f853d480f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573671} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5ccd17-d0df-51f1-89a9-b104c78567ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eca6fcd-90ba-5a2f-8d34-1b77aaafcabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573543} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c71da5-9853-5681-9c08-756d15831853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2177aae9-0cdc-5d1b-af35-640b6cd8ac62" :question/math-academy-id "q-48950" :question/problem "![](images/39/39431059174ad545642732d57f40689e13af962ed2bc091b86eb0d4f41717299.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=-2f(x)+3g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=-2{f}^{′}(x)+3{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=-2{f}^{′}(2)+3{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33082034fa45d3228168431e8263c5a9f2c557ce26a8d637596a1e4e6d47d314.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{2-1}{3-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{3-(-1)}{3-1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =-2{f}^{′}(2)+3{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83470de2-4e61-5a1c-b192-79f1df6d4335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095989} {:answer/id #uuid "95069f73-03b6-5a03-ab67-b16610a1a575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095990} {:answer/id #uuid "b32b8df2-7eab-5d94-a684-c2b38e87a035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095991} {:answer/id #uuid "b5942677-2209-5ef1-b68c-d5e736a62a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095992} {:answer/id #uuid "26718127-638f-5bd0-a44f-9838ff349877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5942677-2209-5ef1-b68c-d5e736a62a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095992} :answer-field/id #uuid "2deafe62-4ea6-5bd2-b1ab-39cbc06aca34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bba9ad1a-f133-594e-8f67-d3021de105b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-73072" :question/problem "![](images/68/68379dee045e8b5d4f123f91c0f8c953d2cbbe6475c5ca13d4435889343c625d.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1.5)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1.5)={f}^{′}(-1.5)\\cdot g(-1.5)+f(-1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1.5).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1.5)=-0.5$ and $g(-1.5)=-0.5.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2.5,0)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1.5)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-0.5)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1.5)=0.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1.5) & ={f}^{′}(-1.5)\\cdot g(-1.5)+f(-1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1.5) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-0.5)+(-0.5)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
